// Test: bugfixes/sem011_capability_conformance_named_message
// Grammar: capability_decl · class_decl.can_clause · function_call
// Semantic: SEM011 (fingerprint 838c14541ba7) — capability conformance is
//   checked at call time. A class value passed to a capability-typed
//   parameter must have declared `can C` (or inherited it). This test
//   pins the POSITIVE case: adding `can Persist` to Session makes the
//   exact call shape from the reporter's minimal_repro compile and run.
//
// Original bug (0.33.86): the reporter noted that Registry.save(Session())
//   without `can Persist` on Session compiled and only trapped at codegen
//   with "no class conforms to capability #268 slot 0 — resolver should
//   have prevented this". That gap was closed in a later patch, but the
//   emitted diagnostic used raw internal SymbolId numbers ("Class#266 does
//   not conform to capability#268") and error code SEM001.
//
// Fix (0.33.109+): src/typechecker/constraint_solver.rs Class→Interface
//   unification looks up class_id and cap_id in the symbol table and
//   emits a SEM011-tagged CompilerError via `semantic_error_with_code`.
//   The message names the class and capability with their declared
//   identifiers and tells the user exactly what to add to the class
//   header.
//
// What this pin protects (both directions):
//   (a) The positive path (this file) compiles and runs — proves the fix
//       did NOT over-reject legitimate `can`-declaring classes.
//   (b) The negative path (removing `can Persist` from Session) MUST
//       still be rejected with SEM011 at check-time; verified manually
//       against the reporter's minimal_repro (see fingerprint 838c14541ba7
//       on the errors dashboard for the exact source).
//
// Fixed in: compiler 0.33.109
//
// Expected output:
//   true

start:
	Session s = Session()
	boolean ok = Registry.save(s)
	print(ok.toString())

can Persist:
	boolean save()
	boolean delete()

class Registry
	functions:
		boolean save(Persist target)
			return target.save()

class Session can Persist
	functions:
		boolean save()
			return true
		boolean delete()
			return true
